%I A400545 #10 Oct 04 2026 18:40:53
%S A400545 4,4,80,16128,858993408,3074457334162219008,
%T A400545 48611766702991209066196372490252451840,
%U A400545 14474011154664524427946373126085988470769712341735039674104210563172558438400
%N A400545 Number of asymmetric Boolean functions of n variables up to cyclic permutations of the variables.
%C A400545 Here asymmetric means that the stabilizer of the Boolean function under the cyclic group C_n is trivial.
%H A400545 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400545/b400545.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..11</a>
%F A400545 a(n) = (1/n)*Sum_{q|n} mu(q)*2^C(n,q) where C(n,q) = (1/q)*Sum_{d|q} phi(d)*2^(n/d).
%F A400545 Here C(n,q) is the number of orbits of the subgroup of order q of C_n acting on the binary n-tuples. Hence 2^C(n,q) is the number of Boolean functions fixed by this subgroup. The formula for a(n) follows by Mobius inversion on the subgroup lattice of C_n.
%e A400545 For n = 3, there are 2^8 = 256 Boolean functions. Of these, 2^C(3,3) = 2^4 = 16 are fixed by the nontrivial rotations. Hence 256 - 16 = 240 functions have trivial stabilizer, forming 240/3 = 80 equivalence classes.
%t A400545 A400545[n_] := DivisorSum[n, MoebiusMu[#]*2^(DivisorSum[#, EulerPhi[#]*2^(n/#) &]/#) &]/n;
%t A400545 Array[A400545, 9] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 04 2026 *)
%Y A400545 Cf. A397652 (all equivalence classes under cyclic permutations of the variables), A000031, A322037.
%K A400545 nonn,new
%O A400545 1,1
